-- Q1: easy / L1 — SOC 3 seal of assurance for marketing
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$A service organization that receives an unqualified SOC 3 report may use the report primarily for which purpose?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Providing detailed control testing results to user auditors for reliance in financial statement audits", "Displaying a seal or summary of assurance on its website to demonstrate trustworthiness to prospective customers", "Submitting to regulatory bodies as evidence of compliance with financial reporting standards", "Replacing the need for a SOC 1 or SOC 2 examination for existing user entities"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AT-C 205 permits a service organization that receives an unqualified SOC 3 report to use a SOC 3 seal and distribute the general-use report broadly, typically on its website, to demonstrate that its controls meet the applicable Trust Services Criteria.
Wrong (A): Detailed control testing results are provided in SOC 2 reports, not in the summarized SOC 3 report.
Wrong (C): SOC 3 reports address Trust Services Criteria, not financial reporting compliance required by regulators.
Wrong (D): SOC 3 reports do not replace the detailed restricted-use SOC 1 or SOC 2 reports needed by user entities and their auditors.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Restriction on using internal auditors for significant risks
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$During the 2025 audit of Greystone Pharmaceuticals, the external auditor considers using the internal audit function to provide direct assistance on testing revenue cutoff procedures. Revenue recognition has been identified as a significant risk. Under AU-C 610, the external auditor should determine that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Internal auditors may not provide direct assistance on any audit procedure", "Internal auditors may provide direct assistance on revenue cutoff because it involves objective testing", "Direct assistance from internal auditors should not be used for procedures that involve significant judgments related to significant risks", "Internal auditors may provide direct assistance only if they hold CPA licenses"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 610.25 restricts the use of internal auditors providing direct assistance for procedures involving significant judgments, especially those related to significant risks. Revenue recognition as a significant risk area involves judgments that should remain with the external auditor.
Wrong (A): Internal auditors may provide direct assistance on many procedures, just not those involving significant judgments on significant risks.
Wrong (B): The nature of the risk, not the objectivity of the procedure, determines whether direct assistance is appropriate.
Wrong (D): CPA licensure is not a requirement under AU-C 610 for internal auditors providing direct assistance.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Discovering component auditor independence impairment mid-engagement
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Midway through the group audit of Galveston Industries, the group engagement partner discovers that Farris & Webb LLP, the component auditor for a subsidiary representing 22% of consolidated revenue, provided tax advisory services to the subsidiary's CFO that impair the component auditor's independence under AICPA ethics rules. Farris & Webb has already completed substantial fieldwork. The group engagement partner should evaluate the situation and determine that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The work already completed by Farris & Webb can be retained since it was performed before the independence issue was discovered", "The group engagement team must assess the nature of the independence threat, determine whether Farris & Webb's work can be relied upon, and if not, arrange for the work to be reperformed by another auditor", "The group engagement team should issue a disclaimer of opinion on the entire consolidated financial statements", "The independence impairment at the component level does not affect the group audit because the group engagement firm itself is independent"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 600.19-.20 requires the group engagement partner to assess the competence and independence of component auditors. When an independence impairment is discovered, the group engagement partner must evaluate whether the impairment affects the reliability of work performed and determine appropriate remediation, which may include having the work reperformed by a different auditor.
Wrong (A): Work performed by a non-independent auditor cannot automatically be retained without evaluating the effect of the impairment.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer is premature if the group engagement team can obtain sufficient evidence through alternative means.
Wrong (D): Component auditor independence is essential; an impairment at the component level affects the group audit evidence.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — User auditor discovers undisclosed subservice organization
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$During the audit of Blackwell Financial, the user auditor discovers through inquiry that PayStream Corp., the service organization for payment processing, recently outsourced its fraud detection function to a third party not mentioned in PayStream's SOC 1 report. The SOC 1 report was prepared using the carve-out method. The user auditor should evaluate this finding and conclude that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The carve-out method already accounts for all subservice organizations, so no further action is needed", "The undisclosed outsourcing requires the user auditor to evaluate completeness of the SOC report description and consider the impact on control risk", "The user auditor should refuse to accept the SOC 1 report entirely and perform all testing directly", "The service auditor bears sole responsibility for identifying and disclosing all subservice organizations"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 402.13 requires the user auditor to evaluate whether the SOC report provides sufficient evidence. Discovering an undisclosed subservice organization means the description of the system may be incomplete. The user auditor must assess the significance of the omitted subservice organization's controls and their potential impact on the user entity's internal control and financial reporting.
Wrong (A): The carve-out method identifies known subservice organizations; an undisclosed arrangement indicates the description may be incomplete.
Wrong (C): The SOC report may still provide useful evidence for other control areas; outright rejection is not the appropriate response.
Wrong (D): While the service auditor describes the system, the user auditor has an independent responsibility to understand the services and evaluate whether the SOC report is sufficient.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Matters component auditor must communicate back to group engagement team
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Burke & Associates serves as the component auditor for a subsidiary of Halcyon Group. During fieldwork, Burke & Associates identifies an instance of fraud involving the subsidiary's purchasing manager. Under AU-C 600, Burke & Associates is required to communicate this finding to the group engagement team because:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Component auditors must share all workpapers with the group engagement team automatically", "Fraud at a component may affect the consolidated financial statements and the group engagement partner's overall risk assessment", "The group engagement team is responsible for disciplining the subsidiary's employees", "Communication is only required if the fraud exceeds component materiality"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 600.41 requires component auditors to communicate matters relevant to the group audit, including fraud or suspected fraud at the component. Fraud may have implications for the consolidated financial statements and may indicate broader risks across the group that the group engagement partner must evaluate.
Wrong (A): Component auditors communicate specific matters, not all workpapers automatically.
Wrong (C): Employee discipline is a management responsibility, not the group engagement team's role.
Wrong (D): Fraud must be communicated regardless of whether it exceeds component materiality, due to its qualitative significance.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Minimum coverage period for a Type 2 SOC report
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$What is the minimum period of time that a SOC 1 Type 2 report must cover for the service auditor to opine on operating effectiveness?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["At least three months of continuous operation", "At least six months of continuous operation", "At least twelve months of continuous operation", "Any period the service organization selects, with no minimum required"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AT-C 320.39 states that the description of tests of controls and their results should cover a period of not less than six months. A period shorter than six months generally would not provide sufficient evidence about operating effectiveness over a meaningful timeframe.
Wrong (A): Three months is too short to provide sufficient evidence of operating effectiveness.
Wrong (C): Twelve months is typical but not the minimum requirement.
Wrong (D): There is a minimum period requirement; the service auditor cannot opine on operating effectiveness for an unreasonably short period.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Who engages the auditor's specialist
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$When the auditor decides to use the work of an auditor's specialist under AU-C 620, who engages the specialist?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The entity's management selects and engages the specialist on behalf of the auditor", "The audit committee must approve and directly engage the specialist", "The auditor or the auditor's firm engages the specialist to assist the auditor", "The specialist self-selects based on the nature of the engagement"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 620.06 defines an auditor's specialist as an individual or organization possessing expertise in a field other than accounting or auditing, whose work is used by the auditor. The auditor or the auditor's firm engages the specialist to provide assistance on audit matters.
Wrong (A): When management engages a specialist, that individual is a management's specialist, not an auditor's specialist.
Wrong (B): The audit committee does not directly engage specialists used by the auditor.
Wrong (D): The auditor selects the specialist based on the engagement's needs; specialists do not self-select.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Monitoring controls at the user entity
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Thornton Industries outsources its inventory management to LogiTrack Services. Thornton implements its own monitoring controls, including daily reconciliation of LogiTrack's inventory reports to Thornton's purchase orders and shipping records. The user auditor should recognize that Thornton's monitoring activities are relevant because they:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Replace the need for a SOC 1 report from LogiTrack", "Provide evidence about the continued operating effectiveness of LogiTrack's controls between SOC report periods", "Are only relevant if LogiTrack does not have a SOC 1 report", "Must be tested by the service auditor as part of the SOC engagement"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 402.11 indicates that the user entity's monitoring of the service organization's activities provides evidence about operating effectiveness, particularly during periods not covered by a SOC report. These monitoring controls help the user auditor assess whether service organization controls continue to operate effectively.
Wrong (A): Monitoring controls complement but do not replace the evidence provided by a SOC 1 report.
Wrong (C): User entity monitoring is relevant regardless of whether a SOC report exists.
Wrong (D): User entity monitoring controls are tested by the user auditor, not the service auditor.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: hard / L3 — Evaluating sufficiency of auditor's specialist qualifications for complex matter
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$The auditor of Pacifica Energy Corp. needs to evaluate a $95 million environmental remediation liability. The auditor engages Dr. Tanaka, an environmental scientist with extensive experience in soil contamination but no experience with groundwater plume modeling, which is the primary driver of the remediation estimate. The auditor should analyze this situation and determine that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Dr. Tanaka's environmental science credentials are sufficient for any environmental remediation matter", "The mismatch between Dr. Tanaka's experience and the specific technical requirements means the auditor should evaluate whether additional or different specialist expertise is needed", "The auditor must engage a second specialist for every environmental liability regardless of the first specialist's qualifications", "Dr. Tanaka should be asked to expand the scope of work to include groundwater modeling regardless of qualifications"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 620.09 requires the auditor to evaluate whether the specialist has the necessary competence, capabilities, and objectivity for the specific matter. General environmental credentials do not automatically extend to all sub-specialties. The auditor must consider whether the specialist's specific expertise matches the technical demands of the engagement.
Wrong (A): Broad credentials in a field do not guarantee competence in every subspecialty within that field.
Wrong (C): A second specialist is not automatically required; the decision depends on whether the first specialist's qualifications are sufficient for the specific matter.
Wrong (D): Asking a specialist to work outside their area of expertise would compromise the quality of the work.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q10: easy / L2 — When a SOC report is necessary
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Ridgemont Corp. uses an external payroll service to process all employee payroll. The payroll service initiates transactions, maintains payroll records, and generates payroll tax filings on behalf of Ridgemont. The user auditor should obtain a SOC 1 report when:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The service organization processes transactions that are significant to the user entity's financial statements", "The user entity pays more than $100,000 annually to the service organization", "The service organization is publicly traded", "A SOC 1 report is required for every outsourced function regardless of significance"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): AU-C 402.07-.08 requires the user auditor to obtain an understanding of the nature and significance of services provided and their effect on internal control relevant to the audit. A SOC 1 report becomes relevant when the service organization processes transactions that are significant to the user entity's financial reporting.
Wrong (B): The dollar amount of the service contract is not the determining factor; significance to financial statements matters.
Wrong (C): The service organization's public trading status is irrelevant to the need for a SOC 1 report.
Wrong (D): A SOC 1 report is not required for every outsourced function — only those significant to financial reporting.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — Component auditor in jurisdiction with different auditing standards
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Constellation Holdings, a U.S.-based group, has a significant subsidiary in a country where the local auditing standards do not require the component auditor to evaluate management's going concern assessment. The group engagement team has identified going concern uncertainty at this subsidiary. The group engagement partner should evaluate the best course of action, which is to:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Accept the component auditor's work as complete because it complies with local standards", "Issue a scope limitation for the entire group audit due to the regulatory difference", "Require the component auditor to perform additional procedures on going concern consistent with GAAS requirements, or perform group-level procedures to address the gap", "Exclude the subsidiary from the group financial statements until local standards are changed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 600.19 requires the group engagement partner to determine whether the component auditor's work is sufficient for group audit purposes. When local standards create a gap relative to GAAS requirements, the group engagement team must arrange for supplemental procedures, either at the component level or through group-level work, to satisfy U.S. auditing standards.
Wrong (A): Compliance with local standards does not automatically satisfy GAAS requirements at the group level.
Wrong (B): A scope limitation is premature when the gap can be addressed through additional procedures.
Wrong (D): Excluding a significant subsidiary from the financial statements is not an appropriate response to an auditing standards difference.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Impact of CUECs not being implemented on audit approach
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Mercer Retail Group uses DataSync Corp. for sales transaction processing. The SOC 1 Type 2 report for DataSync identifies three CUECs, including user entity review of exception reports. During the audit, the user auditor discovers that Mercer's accounting staff never reviews the exception reports. The most likely effect on the audit is that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The user auditor's reliance on DataSync's controls is unaffected because CUECs are optional recommendations", "The control objective related to sales transaction completeness may not be achieved, requiring the user auditor to increase substantive testing in that area", "The service auditor must reissue the SOC 1 report to remove the unimplemented CUECs", "DataSync must implement the controls on behalf of Mercer"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 402.A18 states that CUECs are controls the service organization assumed the user entity would implement. When these controls are not in place, the related control objectives may not be fully achieved, increasing the risk of misstatement. The user auditor must assess the impact and likely increase substantive testing over affected assertions.
Wrong (A): CUECs are integral to achieving the control objectives stated in the SOC report; they are not optional.
Wrong (C): The SOC report does not need to be reissued; the user auditor evaluates the impact of unimplemented CUECs.
Wrong (D): CUECs are the user entity's responsibility to implement, not the service organization's.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Reference to component auditor in group audit report
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$During the group audit of Olympus Enterprises, the group engagement partner considers making reference to the component auditor, Kessler & Dunn LLP, in the group audit report. Kessler & Dunn audited a subsidiary comprising 40% of group assets. The most likely consequence of making reference is that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The group engagement partner transfers all responsibility for the subsidiary's audit to Kessler & Dunn", "The group audit report must include the name of Kessler & Dunn and disclose the portion of the financial statements audited by them, and the group engagement partner's opinion is not modified solely by the reference", "The group engagement partner must issue a qualified opinion whenever reference is made to a component auditor", "Making reference eliminates the need for the group engagement team to perform any group-level procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 600.50-.51 provides that when the group engagement partner decides to make reference to the component auditor, the group audit report identifies the component auditor and indicates the portion of financial statements audited by them. The reference itself does not modify the group opinion and does not transfer the group engagement partner's responsibility for directing and overseeing the group audit.
Wrong (A): Making reference does not transfer responsibility; the group engagement partner retains overall responsibility.
Wrong (C): Reference to a component auditor does not constitute a basis for qualification.
Wrong (D): Group-level analytical procedures and consolidation work remain the group engagement team's responsibility regardless of reference.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Evaluating exceptions noted in a SOC 1 Type 2 report
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$The user auditor for Prescott Manufacturing obtains a SOC 1 Type 2 report for BrightData Systems, Prescott's IT hosting provider. The report contains two exceptions: one access control failed 3 out of 45 times tested, and one change management control failed 2 out of 30 times tested. The user auditor should:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Disregard the SOC report entirely because exceptions make it unreliable", "Evaluate whether the exceptions affect the control objectives relevant to Prescott's audit and determine the impact on assessed control risk and substantive procedures", "Accept the SOC report because the exception rates are below 10%", "Request that BrightData correct the exceptions and reissue the SOC report"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 402.16 requires the user auditor to evaluate the effect of exceptions on the assessment of control risk. The user auditor must determine whether the exceptions affect controls relevant to the user entity's financial reporting and consider whether additional substantive procedures are needed.
Wrong (A): Exceptions do not invalidate the entire report; the user auditor evaluates their significance to the specific audit.
Wrong (C): There is no percentage threshold that makes exceptions automatically acceptable; the auditor must evaluate significance.
Wrong (D): The service organization is not obligated to correct exceptions and reissue the report for the user auditor.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Group engagement team understanding of group-wide controls
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Apex Consolidated operates 12 subsidiaries across different industries. Before assigning work to component auditors, the group engagement team performs procedures to understand the group's entity-level controls. This understanding is required because:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Group-wide controls serve as the sole basis for reducing control risk at all components", "Understanding entity-level controls helps the group engagement team assess group-level risk and determine the work to be performed on components", "Entity-level controls eliminate the need for any component-level testing procedures", "Only component auditors are required to evaluate entity-level controls at each subsidiary"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 600.25-.27 requires the group engagement team to obtain an understanding of group-wide controls and the consolidation process. This understanding informs the group engagement team's risk assessment and helps determine the nature, timing, and extent of involvement in the work performed by component auditors.
Wrong (A): Group-wide controls are one factor in risk assessment, not the sole basis for reducing control risk.
Wrong (C): Entity-level controls complement but do not replace the need for testing at the component level.
Wrong (D): The group engagement team, not just component auditors, must evaluate entity-level controls.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Factors for evaluating internal audit function objectivity
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$When evaluating the objectivity of an entity's internal audit function for purposes of using their work under AU-C 610, the external auditor considers all of the following factors EXCEPT:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Whether the internal audit function's activities are subject to management override", "Whether internal auditors have unrestricted access to the audit committee", "The number of years each internal auditor has worked in public accounting", "Whether the organizational status of the internal audit function supports its ability to be free from bias"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 610.A6-.A8 identifies factors relevant to objectivity, including organizational status, policies to maintain objectivity, management override, and access to the audit committee. Prior public accounting experience of individual internal auditors is not a factor in evaluating the objectivity of the internal audit function.
Wrong (A): Management override is a relevant factor because it can undermine the internal audit function's ability to act on its findings.
Wrong (B): Unrestricted access to the audit committee supports independence and objectivity.
Wrong (D): Organizational status directly affects the internal audit function's ability to be free from bias.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Definition of user entity in SOC context
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$In the context of a SOC 1 engagement, a "user entity" is best defined as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The service organization's internal audit department", "An entity that uses a service organization and whose financial statements are being audited", "Any entity that purchases software from the service organization", "The regulatory agency that reviews the service organization's compliance"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AT-C 320.07 defines a user entity as an entity that uses a service organization and whose financial statements are relevant to the SOC 1 engagement. The user entity relies on the service organization to process transactions or maintain data that affects its financial reporting.
Wrong (A): The service organization's internal audit department is part of the service organization, not a user entity.
Wrong (C): Simply purchasing software does not make an entity a user entity; the relationship involves processing transactions relevant to financial reporting.
Wrong (D): Regulatory agencies oversee compliance but are not user entities in the SOC 1 context.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Restricted-use nature of SOC 1 reports
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$A SOC 1 report is classified as a restricted-use report. This means the report is intended for use by:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Any member of the general public who requests it", "Only the service organization's board of directors", "The service organization, user entities, and user auditors", "Only the regulatory agencies that oversee the service organization"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): AT-C 320.56 specifies that a SOC 1 report is restricted to the service organization, user entities of the service organization, and the user entities' auditors. It is not intended for general distribution because it contains detailed control information relevant only to those parties.
Wrong (A): SOC 1 reports are restricted-use and not available to the general public.
Wrong (B): The report's intended users extend beyond the service organization's board to include user entities and their auditors.
Wrong (D): SOC 1 reports serve user entities and their auditors, not primarily regulatory agencies.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Determining whether a non-significant component requires targeted procedures
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$During the group audit of Landover Corp., the group engagement team identifies that a small subsidiary classified as a non-significant component has recently entered a new line of business with complex revenue arrangements. Although the subsidiary represents only 3% of consolidated revenue, the new revenue contracts involve multiple performance obligations. The group engagement team should analyze this situation and determine that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Non-significant components never require procedures beyond group-level analytical review", "The complexity of the revenue arrangements creates a specific risk that may require targeted audit procedures at the component despite its small size", "The component must be reclassified as significant because it has complex transactions", "The group engagement team should ignore the component until it exceeds 5% of consolidated revenue"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 600.28-.29 requires the group engagement team to identify significant risks regardless of component size. A non-significant component may still require targeted procedures when specific risks exist, such as complex transactions that could give rise to material misstatement at the group level.
Wrong (A): Non-significant components may require targeted procedures when specific risks are identified.
Wrong (C): The presence of complex transactions creates a specific risk but does not automatically reclassify the component as significant.
Wrong (D): There is no minimum revenue threshold below which risks can be ignored; the group engagement team must assess risk factors qualitatively.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Internal audit function performing direct assistance versus the auditor using their work
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$The external auditor of Summit Corp. is considering two approaches for leveraging the internal audit function: using their existing work on accounts payable testing, or having internal auditors provide direct assistance on the current audit. The key distinction between these two approaches that affects the external auditor's supervisory responsibilities is that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["There is no practical difference between the two approaches in terms of supervision", "When using existing work the external auditor evaluates completed procedures; with direct assistance the auditor directs and supervises the work in real time", "Direct assistance requires less supervision because internal auditors follow their own established methodology", "Using existing work always provides more reliable audit evidence than arranging for direct assistance"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 610 distinguishes between using the work of the internal audit function (evaluating completed work for the external auditor's purposes) and direct assistance (internal auditors performing procedures under the external auditor's direction and supervision). Direct assistance involves greater supervisory responsibilities because the external auditor is directing the work in real time.
Wrong (A): The two approaches have significantly different requirements for supervision, direction, and documentation.
Wrong (C): Direct assistance requires more supervision, not less, because the external auditor directs and controls the work.
Wrong (D): Reliability depends on the nature of the procedures, risk level, and degree of judgment, not inherently on the approach used.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Conditions for making reference to a component auditor
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$The group engagement partner for Pacific Holdings is considering whether to make reference to the component auditor of a foreign subsidiary. The component auditor performed the audit using International Standards on Auditing (ISAs) rather than U.S. GAAS. The group engagement partner should analyze whether reference is appropriate by determining that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Reference can be made to any component auditor regardless of the auditing standards applied", "The group engagement partner must first determine whether the component auditor's procedures under ISAs are sufficient for purposes of the group audit under GAAS before deciding to make reference", "Reference automatically requires the component auditor to redo its work under U.S. GAAS", "The use of ISAs prohibits any form of reference to the component auditor in the group audit report"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 600.23 and .50 require the group engagement partner to evaluate whether the component auditor's work is sufficient for the group audit. When the component auditor uses different auditing standards, the group engagement partner must determine whether additional procedures are needed to bridge any gaps before deciding to make reference.
Wrong (A): The group engagement partner must evaluate the sufficiency of the component auditor's work regardless of standards used.
Wrong (C): Reference does not automatically require re-performance; the group engagement partner evaluates sufficiency.
Wrong (D): The use of ISAs does not prohibit reference; it requires evaluation of whether the work meets GAAS requirements.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Definition of service organization under AU-C 402
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Under AU-C 402, a service organization is defined as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Any vendor from which the entity purchases goods or services", "An organization that provides services to user entities that are part of those entities' information systems relevant to financial reporting", "A consulting firm that advises the entity on strategic decisions", "An organization that provides audit services to the entity"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 402.07 defines a service organization as an organization that provides services to user entities that are part of those entities' information systems relevant to financial reporting. The key element is that the service organization's activities affect the user entity's financial reporting process.
Wrong (A): Not all vendors are service organizations; only those whose services are part of the user entity's financial reporting information system.
Wrong (C): Strategic consulting does not involve processing transactions relevant to financial reporting.
Wrong (D): An audit firm provides assurance services, not the type of transaction processing services contemplated by AU-C 402.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Evaluating relevance of management's specialist field of expertise
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Cascade Energy reports a $60 million asset retirement obligation based on estimates from GeoTech Environmental, a specialist in environmental site assessments. The obligation primarily involves decommissioning offshore drilling platforms, which requires specialized marine engineering expertise beyond typical land-based environmental remediation. The auditor should analyze the situation and determine that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Any environmental specialist is qualified to estimate all types of environmental obligations", "The auditor should evaluate whether GeoTech's specific expertise in land-based assessments is relevant to the marine decommissioning estimates, and consider whether additional specialist expertise is needed", "The auditor must automatically reject GeoTech's work because the specialist title does not match the exact obligation type", "Management's choice of specialist cannot be questioned by the auditor"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 500.08 requires the auditor to evaluate the relevance and reasonableness of the specialist's work, including whether their field of expertise is relevant to the specific matter. A specialist's general expertise in environmental assessments may not be sufficient for a highly specialized marine decommissioning estimate.
Wrong (A): Expertise in one area of environmental science does not automatically extend to all environmental matters.
Wrong (C): Automatic rejection is inappropriate; the auditor evaluates whether the specialist's expertise is relevant and sufficient.
Wrong (D): The auditor has a responsibility to evaluate management's specialist, including the relevance of their expertise.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Service organization changes controls after SOC report date
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$During the audit of Monroe Distributing, the user auditor learns that SecureNet, the IT service organization, implemented a major system migration two months after its SOC 1 Type 2 report period ended. The system migration replaced the core application that processes Monroe's transactions. The user auditor should analyze this information and determine that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The SOC 1 report is still fully reliable because it was unqualified during the covered period", "The system migration represents a significant change that may render the SOC report evidence less relevant, requiring the auditor to evaluate the impact and obtain additional evidence about the new system's controls", "System migrations are routine and do not affect the user auditor's reliance on the SOC report", "The user auditor should wait until the next SOC report is issued before completing the audit"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 402.17 requires the user auditor to evaluate changes at the service organization that occur after the SOC report period. A major system migration fundamentally changes the control environment, and the SOC report evidence about the prior system may not be applicable to the new system. The user auditor must assess the impact and obtain appropriate evidence.
Wrong (A): An unqualified SOC report for the prior period does not address controls over a fundamentally different system.
Wrong (C): A major system migration replacing the core application is not routine and significantly affects the control environment.
Wrong (D): Delaying the audit for a new SOC report is generally impractical; the user auditor should perform alternative procedures.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Difference between auditor's specialist and management's specialist
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$The key distinction between a management's specialist and an auditor's specialist is:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Management's specialist must be independent of the entity while the auditor's specialist need not be", "The auditor's specialist is engaged by the auditor to assist with audit evidence, while the management's specialist is engaged by the entity to assist with preparing financial statements or disclosures", "Both terms refer to the same individual but are used in different sections of the audit report", "An auditor's specialist provides services only during interim fieldwork while a management's specialist provides services only at year-end"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 500 addresses management's specialist (engaged by the entity to assist with financial statement preparation), while AU-C 620 addresses the auditor's specialist (engaged by the auditor to assist with obtaining audit evidence). The distinction determines the auditor's responsibilities for evaluating the specialist's work.
Wrong (A): Independence requirements differ — management's specialist need not be independent, but the auditor evaluates their objectivity; the auditor's specialist should be objective.
Wrong (C): They are distinct roles with different relationships to the audit engagement.
Wrong (D): The timing of services is not the distinguishing factor between the two types of specialists.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Factors determining extent of re-performance of internal audit testing
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$The external auditor of Briarwood Corp. plans to use the internal audit function's work on testing controls over fixed asset additions. The internal audit team tested 60 additions using attribute sampling. Under AU-C 610, the external auditor's decision about how many of those 60 items to re-perform is primarily influenced by:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The external auditor must always re-perform 100% of the internal audit function's work", "The assessed risk of material misstatement in the fixed asset area and the degree of subjectivity in evaluating the test results", "Only the size of the internal audit team that performed the testing", "Whether the internal auditors used the same audit software as the external auditor"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 610.18-.19 requires the external auditor to determine the nature and extent of re-performance based on the assessed risk of material misstatement and the degree of subjectivity involved. Higher risk and more subjective evaluations require more extensive re-performance.
Wrong (A): Re-performing 100% is not required; the extent is based on professional judgment considering risk and subjectivity.
Wrong (C): Team size is not a primary factor in determining re-performance extent.
Wrong (D): The audit software used is not a relevant factor in determining re-performance extent.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Multiple SOC reports with different control environments for same service
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Horizon Banking uses PayTech Corp. for loan payment processing. PayTech operates two data centers — one in Texas and one in Oregon — with different control environments. PayTech issues separate SOC 1 Type 2 reports for each data center. The user auditor discovers that Horizon's transactions are processed at both data centers based on load balancing, but PayTech will not disclose which transactions run through which center. The user auditor should evaluate this situation and determine that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The user auditor may rely on whichever SOC report has fewer exceptions", "The user auditor must evaluate both SOC reports because either data center may process any of Horizon's transactions, and assess whether exceptions at either location affect the audit", "The user auditor should select the Texas SOC report because it was issued first", "The inability to determine transaction routing has no impact on the user auditor's assessment"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 402.14-.16 requires the user auditor to evaluate the SOC report as it relates to the user entity's transactions. When transactions may be processed at either location, both control environments are relevant. The user auditor must consider exceptions and findings from both reports in assessing control risk for the user entity's financial reporting.
Wrong (A): Selecting the report with fewer exceptions ignores the possibility that Horizon's transactions are processed at the other location.
Wrong (C): The issuance date does not determine which report is relevant; transaction processing location does.
Wrong (D): The inability to determine routing means both locations must be treated as potentially processing Horizon's transactions.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Agreement on the form of the auditor's specialist report
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$Before engaging an auditor's specialist to assist with the valuation of complex financial instruments, the auditor should reach an understanding with the specialist regarding:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Only the specialist's hourly billing rate, estimated total cost, and payment terms", "The scope and objectives of the work, respective roles and responsibilities, communication protocols, and the form of the specialist's findings", "Whether the specialist will sign the audit report alongside the engagement partner", "Only the deadline for delivering the specialist's final report to the audit team"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 620.11 requires the auditor to agree with the specialist on the nature, scope, and objectives of the work; respective roles; communication protocols; and the form and content of the specialist's report. This understanding ensures the specialist's work meets the auditor's needs.
Wrong (A): Fee arrangements are a practical matter but do not constitute the professional understanding required by the standards.
Wrong (C): Specialists do not sign the audit report; the auditor's report is solely the auditor's responsibility.
Wrong (D): The deadline is one practical element, but the understanding must cover scope, roles, communication, and report format.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Management refuses to allow auditor access to management's specialist
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$During the audit of Wellspring Insurance, the auditor needs to evaluate the reasonableness of a $120 million claims reserve estimated by management's actuary. Management states that the actuary's working papers contain proprietary models and refuses to allow the auditor access to the actuary or the underlying assumptions. The auditor should evaluate this situation and determine that:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The auditor should accept the actuarial estimate at face value because management has the right to protect proprietary information", "Management's refusal to provide access to the specialist's assumptions represents a scope limitation that may affect the auditor's ability to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence, potentially leading to a qualified opinion or disclaimer", "The auditor should engage a different actuary to prepare a completely new estimate without reference to management's actuary", "The auditor should issue an unqualified opinion with an emphasis-of-matter paragraph about the proprietary model"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AU-C 500.08 requires the auditor to evaluate the relevance and reasonableness of a management specialist's assumptions and methods. If management prevents the auditor from accessing the specialist and underlying assumptions, the auditor cannot obtain sufficient appropriate evidence. Under AU-C 705, this scope limitation may result in a qualified opinion or disclaimer of opinion depending on its pervasiveness.
Wrong (A): Proprietary considerations do not override the auditor's need to obtain sufficient evidence about significant estimates.
Wrong (C): Engaging a new actuary may not be practical and does not address the underlying scope limitation regarding management's estimate.
Wrong (D): An unqualified opinion is inappropriate when the auditor cannot evaluate the reasonableness of a significant estimate.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — SOC 3 report general-use purpose
(1, 'Using the Work of Others', $EXPL$How does a SOC 3 report differ from a SOC 2 report in terms of distribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A SOC 3 report is a general-use report that can be freely distributed, while a SOC 2 report is restricted-use", "A SOC 3 report provides more detailed testing results than a SOC 2 report", "A SOC 3 report covers financial reporting controls while a SOC 2 report covers operational controls", "A SOC 3 report can only be distributed to regulators while a SOC 2 report goes to user entities"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): AT-C 205 establishes that a SOC 3 report is a general-use report containing the auditor's opinion on whether the service organization's controls were effective, without the detailed description of tests and results included in a SOC 2 report. SOC 2 reports are restricted-use documents.
Wrong (B): SOC 3 reports contain less detail, not more, compared to SOC 2 reports.
Wrong (C): Both SOC 2 and SOC 3 reports address the Trust Services Criteria, not financial reporting controls.
Wrong (D): SOC 3 reports are general-use and can be distributed to anyone, not just regulators.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
